Canvas: antialiased clipPath (soft clip) and mipmapped reductions

clipPath: Java2D clips to a shape with a hard edge; Android's clipPath edge is antialiased, so every circular avatar and rounded card had a stair-stepped rim. For a non-rectangular clip right after a plain save(), the save becomes an offscreen layer the size of the shape's box (at most 600k px); restore masks it with the shape filled antialiased (DstIn) and draws it back. A second clip in the same save, a larger shape or no save keep the hard clip. MACAPK_SOFT_CLIP=0 = old behaviour. Measured: draw 1.9 -> 2.2-2.9 ms per frame.

Reductions: a strong downscale with one bilinear step reads 4 of every NxN source pixels (aliased edges, glittering texture) -- a 1080 px photo into a 412 px column on a 1x Mac display, an image into an 85 px avatar. Cached scaled blits (dcScaledBlit) now halve the source first (a mipmap chain) and land on the exact size with the last bilinear step; MacApkBitmapShaderPaint picks the mip level nearest the drawn scale from a per-Bitmap (generation-keyed) chain. Both paid once per bitmap and size. MACAPK_MIP_DOWNSCALE=0 = old behaviour.

